latest_response:
Differences in detector energy responses arise because GRB spectra evolve rapidly (typically hardening then softening), so instruments with dissimilar effective areas, energy thresholds, or bandpass shapes record count-rate light curves whose peaks, onsets, or centroids are offset by up to several hundred milliseconds. Background mismatches add further bias: solar-particle or Earth-albedo backgrounds vary with heliocentric distance and orbital geometry, and imperfect subtraction distorts the baseline or wings of weak bursts, shifting cross-correlation peaks by amounts comparable to the statistical timing uncertainty.  

These systematics are mitigated by (1) folding a time-resolved spectral model through each detector’s response matrix to generate instrument-specific synthetic light curves before cross-correlation, (2) restricting analysis to overlapping high-energy bands where response differences are minimized, (3) joint forward-fitting or Bayesian timing models that marginalize over spectral parameters and background components, and (4) empirical cross-calibration derived from bright, spectrally simple bursts observed by multiple spacecraft. Monte-Carlo simulations of the full pipeline then quantify residual timing biases for each event.  

\answer{energy-response & background mismatches shift apparent Δt by ~10–300 ms via spectral evolution & baseline distortion; mitigated by response-folded light-curve modeling, band selection, Bayesian joint fits, & empirical cross-calibration}

