This is the origin of the “~10 %” shorthand: it accurately describes the **best single-experiment result (CMS)** and the **current world-average precision**, while the individual LHCb and ATLAS measurements remain coarser. | These typically quote a world average in the range
\(\mathcal{B} \approx 3.3{-}3.5 \times 10^{-9}\) with a total uncertainty of ≈ 0.3–0.35 × 10^{-9}  
(corresponding to a relative precision of **≈ 9–11 %**).

These numbers confirm that my earlier approximate phrasing (“~5 σ” and “~2 σ”) was a conservative rounding of the 2015 combined result; the exact significance for \(B_s\) is 6.2 σ. | Significances:
